_PRINTF(3)                  Custom C Library                 _PRINTF(3)

NAME
       _printf - write formatted output to standard output

SYNOPSIS
       #include "main.h"

       int _printf(const char *format, ...);

DESCRIPTION
       The _printf function sends formatted output to the standard
       output (stdout) based on the format string specified by the
       first argument.

       The format string is a null-terminated array of characters
       consisting of ordinary characters and conversion specifications
       introduced by the '%' character.

       The conversion specifications supported by _printf are:

       %c
              Prints the character corresponding to the int argument.

       %s
              Prints the null-terminated string pointed to by the
              corresponding argument. If the argument is NULL, behavior
              depends on the implementation (e.g., might print "(null)").

       %%
              Prints a literal '%' character. No additional argument is
              consumed.

       %d, %i
              Prints the signed decimal integer provided as an int
              argument.

       The number and types of additional arguments must match the
       conversion specifiers. Passing incorrect types or missing
       arguments results in undefined behavior.

RETURN VALUE
       Upon successful completion, _printf returns the total number of
       characters written to stdout, excluding the terminating null
       character.

       On failure (e.g., if the format string is NULL or an output error
       occurs), the function returns a negative value (usually -1).

ERRORS
       Possible errors include:

       - format string is NULL, causing _printf to fail and return -1.

       - Unsupported format specifier following '%': behavior may be
         implementation-defined.


CONFORMITY
       _printf is a custom implementation inspired by the standard C
       printf function but limited to the format specifiers listed
       above.

LAST UPDATED
       November 2025

BUGS
       This implementation supports only %c, %s, %%, %i, and %d.
       Behavior for NULL string arguments may differ from standard printf.
       No support for width, precision, or length modifiers.

HISTORY
       This _printf function was created as a custom implementation for
       educational purposes by the Holberton/ALX project in 2025.

NOTES
       Unlike the standard printf, this function does not support float
       or unsigned conversions, and has limited error handling.

EXAMPLES
       _printf("Hello %s\n", "world");
       _printf("Number: %d\n", 42);

